data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Objective**\n\nI'm currently focused on clearly defining the user's objective and constraints. This includes understanding the search for rare, high-value items within a vast space, with a fixed budget, and expensive evaluation. The prompt is multi-layered, and I'm concentrating on the core problem: how to balance exploration vs. exploitation given these limitations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 712,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 124,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Sub-Questions**\n\nI'm now delving into the sub-questions. I am comparing guided vs. unguided exploration with attention to benchmarks. Then I'm looking at mathematical models of idea emergence. Also, I'm examining relevant work in other fields. I'm focusing on the nature of disconnected domains and unstated ideas. I am also working on estimating what remains to be discovered.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 933,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 345,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Falsification Strategies**\n\nI'm now deeply immersed in specifying falsification criteria for each potential answer. I am defining observational, experimental, or data-driven tests, independent of the model's output. The goal is to identify the most cost-effective methods to disprove the claims. I am also working on structuring my response, including strict constraints about epistemic separation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1175,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 587,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Citations and Style**\n\nI'm now implementing rigorous citation practices; every number requires a source, and I'll avoid invented precision, opting for qualitative estimates when exact figures are absent. I'm also ensuring a balanced perspective, including contrary findings where available, and maintaining a dense, scholarly, yet clear French writing style. I understand that I am citing from memory, and making a distinction with standard mathematical theorems that I know conceptually.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1375,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 787,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Implementing the Notation**\n\nI'm now implementing the notational system to distinguish between established knowledge ([Savoir établi]), cited memories ([Cité de mémoire]), and suppositions ([Supposition]). The notation is crucial for maintaining accuracy and transparency. I am also double checking that I adhere to the rule of \"no number without its source,\" especially for empirical claims. I am being very vigilant about this.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1606,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1018,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Memory and Citations**\n\nI'm meticulously double-checking all numerical claims against reliable sources, especially those recalled from memory. I am confirming FunSearch's cap set improvement in dimension 8, aiming for exact source verification. Any figures are cross-referenced before inclusion, and I am marking those from memory accordingly. The memory check has yielded correct results. I am striving for absolute precision in all citations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1998,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1410,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Source Rules**\n\nI am now focusing on the implications of \"aucun chiffre sans sa source.\" The directive is clear: unsourced numerical claims are strictly forbidden. Solid, well-known numbers with sources are acceptable (marked as *cité de mémoire*), and I am avoiding any ambiguous or unverified figures.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2252,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1664,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Search Strategies**\n\nI am now analyzing search strategies without value feedback, including i.i.d. sampling, Monte Carlo, Best-of-N, Pass@k, uniform random walks, random restarts, and Luby restarts without gradient learning, exhaustive search, and grid search. I am exploring scenarios where unguided methods equal or outperform guided ones, notably focusing on No Free Lunch theorems. I'm reviewing Wolpert & Macready's work, and also Igel & Toussaint, and Schumacher, Vose & Whitley, for relevant distinctions, especially concerning the NFL's conditions and limitations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2536,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1948,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Needle-in-Haystack Scenarios**\n\nI am currently examining needle-in-a-haystack scenarios where functions are compressible or of low Kolmogorov complexity, noting that No Free Lunch doesn't apply (Lattimore & Hutter / Streeter). In these cases, guided algorithms can incur computational overhead or risk getting trapped. I'm also considering deceptive landscapes and local traps, as in Goldberg and Whitley's work.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2794,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2206,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Search Efficiency**\n\nI am now assessing the efficiency of various search techniques. I am noting how in high-dimensional spaces with low intrinsic effective dimensionality (e.g., hyperparameter search), i.i.d. random sampling outperforms grid search because random point projections don't overlap, following Bergstra & Bengio (2012). I am contrasting this with scenarios where deceptive local optima trap greedy search, as opposed to i.i.d. sampling or random restarts (Luby et al., 1993).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3089,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2501,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Comparing Search Efficiency**\n\nI am now focusing on contrasting random sampling and grid search, both unguided methods, and how they compare with Bayesian Optimization (BO), particularly in high-dimensional settings where the curse of dimensionality impacts Gaussian Process kernels with limited budgets. I'm exploring scenarios where guided methods outperform unguided ones, especially when regularity, correlation, and substructure (smoothness, RKHS, Lipschitz continuity, modularity) are present.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3356,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2768,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Regret Bounds**\n\nI'm now analyzing regret bounds and best-arm identification. I'm focusing on logarithmic lower bounds for guided strategies (Auer, Cesa-Bianchi & Fischer, 2002) versus linear regret for uniform exploration. Also, I'm noting that adaptive allocations (Audibert, Bubeck & Munos, 2010) reduce error probability more effectively than uniform allocation. Furthermore, I am considering the use of tree search methods (MCTS/UCT) and hierarchical structure.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3627,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3039,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Tree Search**\n\nI'm now focusing on tree search. I've noted Coquelin & Munos's 2007 result showing UCT's hyper-exponential runtime on malicious trees. I'm also considering evolutionary algorithms' time complexity: unimodal functions are handled efficiently, but jump functions are difficult. I'm exploring heavy-tailed mutation benefits, along with traps that make EAs perform worse than chance.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3905,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3317,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Diversity Benefits**\n\nI'm now examining the advantages of quality-diversity algorithms, particularly MAP-Elites (Mouret & Clune, 2015) and novelty search (Lehman & Stanley, 2011). I am thinking that in deceptive landscapes (e.g., mazes with dead ends), behavioral novelty outperforms both objective-guided and random search. Also, I am thinking about recently published results that I can compare, at equal budget, in automated discovery.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4172,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3584,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Ablation Studies**\n\nI'm now integrating recent ablation studies. I find that island evolution outperforms no-feedback sampling, per the FunSearch cap set result. I'm noting AlphaEvolve's (Novikov et al., 2025) superior performance, even though I'll be careful about dates and numbers. I'm assessing this in the context of recent results and considering recent benchmarks.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4443,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3855,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Comparing Search Strategies**\n\nI am now comparing guided and unguided search methods more directly, specifically focusing on how guided strategies, like those used in automated theorem proving (Lample et al., 2022), can surpass unguided ones. I am also exploring examples like AlphaCode (Li et al., 2022) that leverage massive unguided sampling for initial solution generation, highlighting the significance of both approaches. I'm noting the performance of Symbolic Regression methods as well.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4733,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4145,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Equation Discovery**\n\nI'm now examining how guided search, as seen in symbolic regression (e.g., Udrescu & Tegmark, 2020), surpasses unguided brute-force within budget constraints. Specifically, I'm analyzing how guided approaches using symmetries or physics (like AI Feynman) achieve superior results compared to random search. Even exhaustive symbolic regression can be competitive with the appropriate constraint.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5002,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4414,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Sampling Efficiency**\n\nI'm now integrating recent findings on sampling efficiency. I'm noting Snell, Lee, Xu & Kumar (2024) and Brown et al. (2024), which show that, at equal compute budgets, i.i.d. sampling (Best-of-N / Pass@k) can surpass tree-search/PRM-guided approaches on hard problems or with poorly calibrated reward models. The paper *Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally can be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters* indicates power law coverage, where parallel revisions are competitive with PRM-guided lookahead search.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5267,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4679,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Comparing Resampling Strategies**\n\nI'm now comparing resampling strategies, particularly in situations with imperfect verifiers where false positives hinder gains, according to Stroebl et al. (2024). I'm also finding that simple random search can outperform guided methods in neural architecture search (Li & Talwalkar, 2020; Sciuto et al., 2020), and that random search excels at reinforcement learning, per Mania et al. (2018).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5528,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4940,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Search Tradeoffs**\n\nI'm now diving into the work of Tang, Yang et al., comparing automated theorem proving via LLMs, where Pass@K sampling rivals MCTS. I'm also finding that Bayesian Optimization's edge over random search diminishes with noise, as shown in the Black-Box Optimization Challenge 2020. I'll consider how these comparisons change with budget size.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5817,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5229,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Budget-Aware Strategies**\n\nI'm now analyzing the impact of budget size on search strategy efficacy, particularly in low-budget, high-dimensional, unstructured scenarios. I'm exploring methods like Hyperband, combining pure random sampling and adaptive budget allocation with early stopping. I'm focusing on contrasting these budget-aware approaches with pure Bayesian Optimization across various regimes.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6083,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5495,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Novelty Dynamics**\n\nI'm now examining how the introduction of new elements in a system relates to Heaps' law, Zipf's law, and temporal correlations, as seen in the work of Tria et al. (2014) and Monechi et al. (2017). I'm noting the model's predictions of sublinear novelty growth and power-law frequency distributions. I am exploring validation using datasets from Wikipedia, Twitter, and scientific patents.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6380,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5792,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Innovation Dynamics**\n\nI'm now examining how simple urn models overlook concept topology, as noted by Opini, Milojević & Latora (2018), who propose a reinforced random walk on networks, which they tested on scientific articles and patents. I'm also considering Gerlach & Altmann's (2013) critiques, which remind me that multiple uncorrelated mechanisms can also produce Heaps and Zipf, making an adjustment to those alone non-unique.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6636,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6048,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Idea Emergence**\n\nI'm now modeling how new ideas emerge from combinations of existing ones. The super-exponential growth potential, $C_m(I)$, is unbounded. The limitation is processing and verification, as in the prompt's context. I am also investigating the *knowledge networks / hypergraphs* aspect.\n\nI'm looking at Uzzi, Mukherjee, Stringer & Jones (2013)'s work on atypical combinations and scientific impact, a null model from a graph with millions of Web of Science articles. Higher-impact articles, I'm noting, often merge typical and rare combinations. Also, I'm considering critiques by Boyack & Klavans (2014) and Wang, Veugelers & Stephan (2017).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6925,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6337,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Combinatorial Novelty**\n\nI am now closely examining the implications of combinatorial novelty in scientific discovery. Recent findings indicate that novel combinations exhibit higher variance, delayed recognition, and potentially unfavorable biases in high-impact journals (Fontana, Iori, Montobbio & Sinatra, 2020). Moreover, various novelty measures show weak correlations, emphasizing the complexity of this concept. I am also considering phase transitions in my thinking.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7134,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6546,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Network Structures**\n\nI'm now analyzing the implications of network structures for discovery. Rzhetsky et al. (2015) show how exploring long-distance connections, rather than focusing on local consolidation, accelerates collective discovery. I am noting a phase transition in efficiency as network density changes. Also, I am considering phase transitions in problem spaces, as per Cheeseman et al. (1991) and Monasson et al. (1999) in the context of constraint satisfaction.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7377,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6789,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Phase Transitions**\n\nI'm now focusing on phase transitions in the context of discovery, specifically algorithmic difficulty peaks coinciding with the transition where rare solutions cluster, isolated by energy barriers, according to Cheeseman et al. and Monasson et al. I'm also examining how Kauffman & Weinberger's NK landscapes, adapted for organizational innovation by Levinthal and Fleming & Sorenson, can be viewed through the lens of those concepts.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7618,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7030,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating NK Landscapes**\n\nI'm now focusing on NK landscapes. Empirical tests by Fleming and Sorenson (2001, 2004) validate the model's predictions on US patents, revealing an inverted U-shaped relationship between interdependence ($K$) and invention value. Scientific literature improves search when $K$ is high. There is a challenge of a fixed dimension, but it yields valuable insights.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7883,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7295,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Economic Returns**\n\nI'm now integrating economic models of research, specifically extreme value models. I'm focusing on Evenson & Kislev (1976) and Kortum (1997), emphasizing the \"fishing out\" concept and diminishing returns in research. The prompt's context aligns with this, given the need for increasingly expensive efforts. The current frontier height and new ideas shift are key considerations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8130,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7542,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Research Productivity**\n\nI am now focusing on research productivity and its economic implications, particularly Diminishing Returns, with citations from Jones (1995) and Bloom, Jones, Van Reenen & Webb (2020). These models suggest decreasing research productivity over time as $\\phi \u003c 1$ within the equation $\\dot{A} = \\alpha L_A^\\lambda A^\\phi$. I am also cross-referencing industry-specific data (semiconductors, agriculture, healthcare, etc.) and noting the increase in researchers required to maintain output (i.e. Moore's Law, Bloom et al., 2020).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8421,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7833,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Economic Models**\n\nI'm now integrating economic models of research and analyzing citations regarding research productivity. I'm noting the magnitude of the research output (*cité de mémoire*), including its decline, and cross-referencing industry-specific data. I'm also considering contradictory research that suggests the effect of knowledge accumulation and increased specialization. Furthermore, I am assessing and evaluating counter-studies regarding disruptive innovation trends.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8662,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8074,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Contrasting Results**\n\nI'm now integrating contrasting economic findings on declining disruptive innovation, per recent work. Studies by **Holst et al. / Petersen, Arroyave & Pammolli (2024/2025)** and **Weder (2024)** demonstrate that the decline in disruptive patents might be an artifact. I'm focusing on the effect of truncated citations and the inflation of bibliographies. This contrasts with earlier findings.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8983,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8395,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Scientific Impact**\n\nI am now focusing on the comparison of research funding models. The study by **Azoulay, Graff Zivin, Li (2010)** analyzes the impact of HHMI fellowships (long-term, flexible funding) versus NIH grants (short-term, predefined deliverables). HHMI fellows produce significantly more highly-cited articles, but also more failures. This contrasts with earlier studies.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9220,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8632,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Citation Cascades**\n\nI'm now integrating citation cascade modeling to reflect scientific impact's stochastic nature, contrasting single-genius myths. I'm focusing on branch processes, particularly Barabási & Albert's preferential attachment model and Wang, Song & Barabási's (2013) refined Poisson model for scientific impact. I am noting how the models are tested, with Ke, Ferrara, Radicchi & Flammini's work on \"Sleeping Beauties\" as a further consideration.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9507,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8919,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Idea Spread**\n\nI am now focusing on the modeling of idea generation and spread. I'm noting that new models of *epidemiology of ideas* focus on branching processes (**Goffman & Newill, 1964; Bettencourt et al., 2006**). I am contrasting these results with my earlier models. I'm also considering the individual productivity model, $c = Q \\cdot p$ (**Sinatra et al., 2016**).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9790,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9202,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Success Patterns**\n\nI'm now examining success patterns, contrasting uniform probability of career success with \"hot streak\" observations. I'm noting studies by Liu et al. that link major achievements to temporal clusters, with exploration followed by exploitation. This contrasts with my prior models. I'll consider how this affects the question of combinatorial novelty.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10025,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9437,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Differentiating Observation and Narrative**\n\nI'm now separating measured data from narratives, particularly in the context of scientific philosophy. I'm focusing on the distinction between the \"context of discovery\" and the \"context of justification\" based on work by Reichenbach and Popper, highlighting their skepticism about a logic of discovery, and contrasting this with Peirce's concept of abduction.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10303,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9715,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Synthesizing Methodological Shifts**\n\nI am now focusing on the philosophical underpinnings and methodological shifts, I am integrating the ideas of Hanson, Simon, Kuhn, Lakatos, and Feyerabend, distinguishing between the context of discovery and justification. I'm noting that while Popper's skepticism of a logic of discovery, and that Peirce's abduction suggests a best inference. I'm also planning to integrate Wray's bibliometric tests on Kuhn.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10556,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9968,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Scientific Transitions**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from social epistemology regarding scientific revolutions, citing that these transitions appear more gradual and hybrid than sudden, incommensurable breaks, according to studies of citations. I'll also be considering statistical physics.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10820,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10232,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Neural Activity**\n\nI am now assessing the relationship between network dynamics and creativity. I'm focusing on the relationship between Default Mode Network and cognitive control networks, as measured by Beaty et al. (2016, 2018), and contrasting this with insights on algorithm phase transitions. I'm also considering the implications of brain network dynamics and creative cognition.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 11113,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10525,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Neural Correlates**\n\nI'm now integrating neural correlates of creativity, particularly regarding divergent thinking and \"Aha!\" moments. I'm focusing on the interplay of the Default Mode Network and Executive Control Network. I'm noting studies by Jung-Beeman, Lacaux, and Beaty. I'm also considering the implications of brain network dynamics and creative cognition.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 11328,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10740,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Sleep and Insight**\n\nI'm now examining the impact of sleep on insight, specifically noting Wagner et al.'s (2004) research on N1 sleep and its effect on finding hidden rules, a result that more than doubled the success rate compared to wakefulness or deeper sleep. However, I am also noting the limitations of these lab studies, since they measure short-term creativity and do not measure long-term scientific discovery.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 11615,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 11027,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Replication Challenges**\n\nI'm now grappling with the challenges of replicating creativity-related neural correlates, citing Dietrich & Kanso (2010), which highlighted heterogeneity and poor replication. I'm also addressing the weak effects of incubation periods, based on Sio & Ormerod's (2009) meta-analysis. The work by Schönauer et al. (2018) casts doubt on the replicability of some results.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 11888,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 11300,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Incubation Dynamics**\n\nI'm now comparing cognitive-load incubation results. I find that mind-wandering enhances divergent thinking, per Baird et al. (2012), which contrasts with Smeekens & Kane (2016) and Murray et al. (2024), who failed to replicate this effect. I am noting the impact of mental fixation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 12160,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 11572,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Einstellung Effects**\n\nI'm now integrating the Einstellung effect and analogical reasoning into the model. I'm noting that **Luchins (1942)** and **Duncker (1945)** show that familiar methods can block novel solutions. Also, **Gentner (1983)** and **Gick & Holyoak (1980, 1983)** reveal the rarity of spontaneous analogical transfer without explicit cues. I'm focusing on **Dunbar (1995, 1997)**'s work on in vivo observation of laboratories, since it can ground my claims.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 12419,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 11831,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Laboratory Discovery**\n\nI am now focusing on findings from direct recordings of molecular biology labs. I'm noting that discoveries often arise from unexpected results or experimental anomalies. Also, analogies used in real time are mostly local, and reasoning is distributed within the lab group, according to my research.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 12678,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 12090,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Expert Variation**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the models to show that \"blind\" search is constrained by expert knowledge. The experts' variations are not purely random, as Dasgupta et al. show. Further, I am considering Wallas' (1926) four-stage model of the thought process, inspired by Poincaré and Helmholtz. I am also investigating work on external representations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 12944,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 12356,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating External Representations**\n\nI'm now focusing on how constraints affect problem-solving. External representations significantly impact error rates and solution paths; encoding constraints externally (notation) versus relying on internal memory yields different results. I am looking at Landy & Goldstone (2007) and how spatial arrangement influences structure perception.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 13209,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 12621,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Metaphors and Notations**\n\nI'm now integrating Lakoff and Johnson's work on embodied metaphors and Lakoff and Núñez's perspective on mathematics. I am noting historical examples of the impact of notation, specifically contrasting Leibniz's notation with Newton's, and considering this influence on differential calculus. While this argument is difficult to isolate from institutional factors, the effect is real.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 13477,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 12889,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Neutral Networks**\n\nI'm now exploring the concept of neutral networks and exaptation in biological systems. Specifically, I'm considering how functionally equivalent genotypes can form vast networks, which allows neutral drift to facilitate access to novel functions, citing **Schuster, Fontana, Stadler & Hofacker (1994)**. The exaptation concept, as outlined by **Gould & Vrba (1982)**, is also being examined, drawing support from paleontological and molecular evidence.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 13769,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 13181,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Innovation Analogy**\n\nI am now extending my research to include long-term experimental evolution findings by Blount, Borland & Lenski (2008), focusing on the dependency on prior mutations. I am also considering Ohno's (1970) work on gene duplication. I'm noting that while applying these biological principles to scientific discovery (Dawkins' memetics, Popper/Campbell/Toulmin's evolutionary epistemology) is useful, idea selection isn't strictly Darwinian, but Lamarckian.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 14036,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 13448,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Memory Models**\n\nI'm now refining the models to show that \"blind\" search is constrained by expert knowledge. The experts' variations are not purely random, as Dasgupta et al. show. I am also considering Wallas' (1926) four-stage model of the thought process, inspired by Poincaré and Helmholtz. I am also investigating work on external representations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 14277,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 13689,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Discovery Narrative**\n\nI am now focusing on contrasting idealized discovery narratives with the reality of scientific practice, particularly emphasizing the iterative, error-prone nature of discovery, as opposed to sudden insights. I am also thinking about **Merton & Barber (2004)**'s *Serendipity* book and documenting accidental anomalies. Finally, I'm noting the distortions introduced by heroic and teleological accounts.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 14566,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 13978,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Revising Memory Models**\n\nI am now revising memory models to integrate findings on the iterative and error-prone nature of discovery. This is in contrast to idealized accounts. I'm focusing on contrasting narratives with real scientific practice. I'm considering **Merton & Barber (2004)** and the role of serendipity. I am also thinking about distortions in accounts of \"heroic\" discovery.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 14837,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 14249,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Dream Inspiration**\n\nI'm now examining dream-inspired scientific discoveries, noting that while Srinivasa Ramanujan claimed inspiration from the goddess Namagiri, his notebooks also reveal trial-and-error exploration. I am also considering Alexandre Grothendieck's contrast between direct and indirect approaches to problem-solving. Finally, Kary Mullis's account of PCR invention is reviewed.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 15127,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 14539,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Replication Dynamics**\n\nI'm now grappling with the challenges of applying dream-inspired discovery accounts in research, while contrasting these with systematic trial-and-error exploration. Srinivasa Ramanujan's inspiration versus his notebooks and Mullis' PCR account are considered. Also, I am contrasting direct vs. indirect problem-solving approaches of Grothendieck and evaluating how memory impacts these narratives, drawing on my previous thoughts.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 15421,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 14833,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Idea Emergence**\n\nI am now focusing on analyzing idea emergence in disconnected domains and its success factors. I am differentiating between what increases the probability of an idea's appearance versus its eventual success, particularly in scenarios involving disjoint literature, referencing Burt's work on structural holes as a positive influence on both appearance and fruition. I am distinguishing these ideas from established knowledge or those that are my own.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 15681,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 15093,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Synthesizing Interdisciplinary Ideas**\n\nI am now synthesizing the interdisciplinary knowledge on idea generation, focusing on how individuals at the interface of disconnected groups generate valuable insights, as shown in the American Journal of Sociology. I'm investigating thematic mobility, considering hybrid researchers, and formal translatability. I'm noting shared lexicons, mathematics, and software that enable the recognition of structural isomorphisms.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 15915,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 15327,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Literature Review**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from the LBD literature, citing Swanson & Smalheiser's (1997) work, and Tshitoyan et al. (2019)'s study on unsupervised word embeddings. I am focusing on how semantic search tools aid trans-domain discovery. I'm also considering the implications of risk-tolerant funding models and informal spaces.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 16156,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 15568,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Interdisciplinary Barriers**\n\nI am now assessing challenges to interdisciplinarity. I'm noting the re-emergence of ideas across fields (**retropagation, méthode de Laplace/inférence variationnelle**), the funding penalties for interdisciplinary research (**Bromham et al., 2016**), and citation/publication biases (**Wang et al., 2017**). I am focusing on coordination costs of verification.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 16413,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 15825,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Interdisciplinary Tradeoffs**\n\nI am now focusing on the tradeoffs in interdisciplinary work. I'm noting that Leahey, Beckman & Stanko's 2017 study reveals a visibility increase, but also productivity reduction due to higher costs. I'm also considering the implications of \"analogical false friends,\" potentially leading to alluring but inaccurate ideas.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 16656,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 16068,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Interdisciplinary Frameworks**\n\nI'm now integrating factors that influence idea emergence, focusing on accessibility, standardization, and formalization of published results. Open data, reproducible code, and unified formal libraries (like Mathlib in Lean) streamline verification. Also, syntheses, critical reviews, and unifying reformulations reduce deductive distance, making remote consequences more immediate. Systematic deduction, generation, and meta-analysis tools (like Sourati & Evans, 2023) are also being considered.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 16893,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 16305,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Information Overload**\n\nI am now focusing on the implications of information overload, canonization, and the Matthew effect, as shown by Chu & Evans (2021) in PNAS, which is *cité de mémoire*. When the annual article volume is high, researchers increasingly rely on a small core of ultra-cited papers, exploring the periphery less. Also, negative results and anomalies are often unreported. I am considering these factors, since they reduce the appearance and fruition of novel ideas.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 17133,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 16545,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Cognitive Bias**\n\nI'm now analyzing shared, implicit presuppositions and their impacts, noting that overlooked consequences result from such biases. This resembles a collective Einstellung effect, blocking simple violations of a tacit assumption. I am now considering historical examples of discovery.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 17376,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 16788,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Chain-Deduction Risks**\n\nI am now focusing on the problems in science: cascading publications to reach remote implications increases error risk. The exponential decrease in reliability is very discouraging, leading to disincentives in replication, a lack of prestige for unsexy results, and fear of being scooped. Also, verification costs and impractical experiments come into focus.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 17632,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17044,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Discovery Constraints**\n\nI'm now analyzing the constraints to discovery, noting that implications can be distant or deemed unimportant. I am also working on estimating what remains to be discovered. I am considering the two core problems here: (A) Estimating what remains to be discovered, and (B) Deciding the next research steps.\n\n\\n\\nI'm now focusing on estimating what remains to be discovered, a classic statistical and decision-theoretic problem. I'm exploring the problem of estimating the probability of pulling an unseen object (missing mass) and the use of the Good-Turing estimator, introduced by Good in 1953, with a credit to Alan Turing.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 17890,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17302,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Novelty Discovery**\n\nI'm now implementing the Good-Turing estimator and its improvements to gauge the potential for discovering novel, high-value items, focusing on filtered discovery streams. Orlitsky, Suresh & Wu's work on smoothed estimators is particularly relevant for extrapolating discovery rates. I am testing the reliability of these methods to predict the value of rare discoveries.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 18198,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17610,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Missing Discoveries**\n\nI am now focusing on estimating total hidden items using the Chao1 lower bound and capture-recapture methods. These non-parametric methods are being used to estimate the number of undiscovered items, particularly under independent generators. I am evaluating Chao's lower bound and capture-recapture models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 18470,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17882,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Discovery Limits**\n\nI'm now grappling with the fundamental limits of discovery, particularly the non-identifiability of the total number of hidden items without assumptions on minimal probability. The Good-Turing estimator and Chao1 are highly sensitive to semantic equivalence assumptions, given Mao and Lindsay (2007). I'm also now shifting focus to value estimation via extreme value theory (EVT).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 18751,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18163,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Statistical Tools**\n\nI am now focusing on extreme value theory (EVT). I am integrating the Pickands-Balkema-de Haan model, also known as the Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD), as it estimates the probability of record-breaking values and expected gains. I am also considering Bayesian non-parametric models, especially Dirichlet and Pitman-Yor processes.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19040,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18452,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Discovery Rate Bounds**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on discovery rate bounds and the limitations of extrapolation techniques. I am noting how *i.i.d.* sampling assumptions affect estimators and the need for importance sampling in guided searches, which is discussed by Chen et al. I'm also finding that when a generator is fixed, and the search is *guided*, then the assumptions break down. I'm also noting that extrapolating outside the support can fail when there's a barrier.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19360,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18772,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Search Stopping**\n\nI'm now focusing on determining when to halt the research, allocating resources among generators, and considering different methods. I am reviewing optimal stopping rules, the Pandora's Box problem from Weitzman (1979), and its application to known value distributions. The constraints of known and independent distributions are central to the analysis. I am also investigating optimal stopping with unknown distributions using Bayesian models, focusing on the value of information.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19582,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18994,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Research Focus**\n\nI'm now integrating techniques for stopping and resource allocation, I'm focusing on myopic optimal stopping rules and their limitations, particularly Gittins' index and Charnov's marginal value theorem. My emphasis is on adapting my resource allocation strategy for the given budget. I am also investigating optimal stopping with unknown distributions using Bayesian models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19857,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19269,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Extreme Search Strategies**\n\nI'm now diving into extreme bandit problems for multi-generators within a fixed budget. I'm focusing on strategies beyond traditional UCB, which are suboptimal for extreme value maximization. I'm prioritizing generators with heavier tails, leveraging extreme value theory and algorithms like ExtremeHunter, which is based on Fréchet/Pareto tails.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20121,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19533,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Budget Allocation**\n\nI'm now refining multi-generator resource allocation with a fixed budget, focusing on approaches beyond UCB. I'm prioritizing heavy-tailed generators and implementing algorithms like ExtremeHunter, building on Fréchet/Pareto tail models. I am also investigating the Good-Turing bandit algorithms, incorporating work from Bubeck, Ernst & Garivier (2013).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20410,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19822,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Black Swans**\n\nI'm now analyzing the implications of \"Black Swan\" events and out-of-support extrapolations, noting the zero estimate for generators failing to produce high-value items after *n* trials. There is also the cost of estimation vs. a limited budget and generator correlation. I'm focusing on these aspects.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20673,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 20085,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

