data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Key Citations**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing and confirming citations critical to my economic models of research. I am implementing the following: Evenson & Kislev (1976), Kortum (1997), Jones (1995), Bloom, Jones, Van Reenen & Webb (2020), Jones (2009), Jones & Weinberg (2011), and Park, Leahey & Funk (2023). I am checking for precise bibliographic information.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 29189,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 28601,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Recent Citations**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing and confirming citations. I have added C. & Weder's 2024 work on disruptive patents, Manso's (2011) study on motivating innovation, and Azoulay et al. (2011) on incentives in academic life sciences. I've also incorporated the sociology of science, including Merton (1961), Simonton (1979), Wang et al. (2013), and Ke et al. (2015). Sinatra et al. (2016) is also being reviewed.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 29507,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 28919,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Discovery Models**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on career hot streaks (**Liu et al., 2018, 2021**) and the epidemiology of ideas (**Bettencourt et al., 2006**) into my discovery model. These results inform my understanding of success patterns and the spread of new concepts, contrasting uniform probabilities with observed temporal clusters. Also, I am now focused on the context of discovery, justification, and key thinkers (**Reichenbach, Popper, Hanson, Simon, Kuhn, Lakatos, Feyerabend**).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 29805,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 29217,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Critical Citations**\n\nI'm now implementing and verifying key citations, including Lakatos (1970) and, based on prior notes, confirming Sio & Ormerod (2009)'s meta-analysis, Lacaux et al. (2021) on sleep, Beaty et al. (2016, 2018), Jung-Beeman et al. (2004), Wagner et al. (2004), and Dietrich & Kanso (2010), alongside other references that I had previously listed.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 30139,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 29551,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Incubation Models**\n\nI'm now integrating previous meta-analyses (Sio and Ormerod, 2009) and contrasting incubation period results, particularly mind-wandering effects (Baird et al., 2012; Smeekens & Kane, 2016). I'm also including Einstellung effects, as well as the work of Luchins (1942), Duncker (1945), Gentner (1983), Gick & Holyoak (1980), and Dunbar (1995) to better characterize the iterative nature of the process.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 30423,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 29835,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Model Integration**\n\nI am now focusing on integrating research on scientific reasoning within lab settings, including the role of blind variation, selective retention (Campbell, Simonton), and iterative thought processes (Wallas). Also, I'm integrating work on how external representations (Zhang & Norman) and metaphors (Thibodeau & Boroditsky, Lakoff & Johnson) shape thinking, specifically focusing on how they can be used to describe the findings in the previous section.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 30753,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 30165,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Evolutionary Analogies**\n\nI'm now extending the discussion to evolutionary biology. I'm focusing on **Fontana, Schuster, Stadler & Hofacker (1994)**'s work on neutral networks and exaptation, using the RNA model. Then, I am integrating the concept of exaptation from **Gould & Vrba (1982)**. I am also considering **Blount, Borland & Lenski (2008)**'s work on historical contingency. And I'm also bringing in **Ohno's (1970)** work on gene duplication. I am also thinking about work on scientific practices **Galison (1997), Holmes (1985), Gruber (1974) and Merton & Barber (2004)**\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 31110,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 30522,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Historical Narratives**\n\nI'm now integrating historical and biographical details to contrast discovery narratives, and including cited sources. I am cross-referencing *The Whig Interpretation of History* with specific examples from Poincaré, Hadamard, Wotiz & Rudofsky, Grothendieck, Mullis, Rabinow, Keller, and Hardy. I'm also including works on knowledge discovery, such as Swanson, Burt, and Wuchty.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 31419,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 30831,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Knowledge Integration**\n\nI'm now integrating Bromham et al. (2016) on funding penalties with Chu & Evans (2021) on canonization and my Good-Turing model. I'm focusing on the interplay between interdisciplinarity, limited funding, and diminished novelty. I am also planning to bring in Good & Toulmin (1956). I'm also planning to bring in the work of Ioannidis (2005) and Open Science Collaboration (2015).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 31720,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 31132,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Exploration Strategies**\n\nI am now focusing on various estimators to find novel ideas, drawing from statistical and decision-theoretic models. I'm exploring the Good-Turing estimator, and its recent improvements, to anticipate and characterize potential novel discoveries. I'm also exploring the Pickands-Balkema-de Haan model, also known as the Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD), as it estimates the probability of record-breaking values and expected gains.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 32057,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 31469,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Stopping and Allocation**\n\nI'm now integrating optimal stopping and resource allocation techniques. I'm focusing on adapting my strategies for different budget constraints and am cross-referencing Weitzman's Pandora's Box problem and its variants (Weitzman, 1979; Streeter & Smith, 2006; Bubeck et al., 2013). I'm also planning to incorporate Nemhauser et al. (1978) on submodular optimization, and Charnov (1976) and Carpentier & Valko (2014) on stopping rules.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 32327,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 31739,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Methodology Notes**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the notational system to distinguish direct verifiability ([Savoir établi]), cited memories ([Mémoire]), and suppositions ([Supposition]). I'm also confirming that no empirical number is presented without a source, opting for qualitative estimates when exact figures are uncertain. This strict adherence to methodology is crucial for accuracy.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 32517,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 31929,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Structure and Falsification**\n\nI am now focusing on structuring the response to explicitly separate the factors influencing the *appearance* versus the *fruition* of ideas across the diverse disciplines. The goal is to provide a falsifier for each section, emphasizing cost-effective tests that can validate or refute claims independently of any model output. I'm also conducting a thorough audit to remove all unsourced numbers and specific values.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 32824,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 32236,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Date Accuracy**\n\nI'm now carefully verifying dates and specific numerical claims in line with my rigorous citation standards. I am adding citations to specific dates: for example, Einstein (1916) for gravitational waves and adding citations for dates such as 1964. Also, I'm avoiding casual percentages like \"top 1%\" and opting for qualitative statements or citations. Finally, I'm structuring data to support my falsification tests.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 33128,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 32540,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "5wgDa_GDM--go_UPwpLE2QE"}

